<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taygetus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Taygetos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Taygetus is the highest mountain range of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, rising to 2,404 metres at Profitis Ilias. It runs north to south between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a> and Messenia and towers over the site of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> to the west. The range was the barrier that Spartan armies crossed to conquer Messenia, and later writers told how Spartans exposed weak infants in a chasm on its slopes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taygetus.jpg" alt="Taygetus" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Herbert Ortner, Vienna, Austria · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taygetos_Ilias_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Taygetus massif is about 100 kilometres long, running from the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> to Cape Matapan, its southernmost point. It overlooks <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalamata">Kalamata</a> and lies within the prefectures of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcadia">Arcadia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a> and Messenia. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurotas">Eurotas</a> has some of its headwaters in the northern part of the range, and the slopes are heavily forested, mainly with Greek fir and black pine.</p><p>The name is one of the oldest recorded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, appearing in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a>, and in myth it came from the nymph Taygete. In Byzantine times and until the 19th century the mountain was also called Pentadaktylos, Greek for five-fingered. Its highest summit, Profitis Ilias, is probably the classical Mount Taleton mentioned by Pausanias. Geologically the range is a limestone horst beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurotas">Eurotas</a> rift valley, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> fault below its eastern face caused the earthquake of 464 BCE that levelled Sparta.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The slopes have been inhabited since at least Mycenean times, and near the village of Arna the site of Arkina has three beehive tombs. The mountain was one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>'s natural defences. The Spartans threw criminals into a chasm called Ceadas, and remains of adults found there by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-and-kapodistrian-university-of-athens">University of Athens</a> appear to confirm it as a place of punishment for criminals, traitors and captives.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> wrote that Spartan elders judged newborn babies and left those thought unfit at the foot of Taygetus. No other ancient source says this, and the bones found at the site belonged to adolescents and adults, not infants. In the age of the barbarian invasions the mountain sheltered the native population, and many villages on its slopes date from then. In Medieval times the citadel and monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mystras">Mystras</a> was built on its steep slopes and became a centre of Byzantine civilisation and the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/despotate-of-the-morea">Despotate of the Morea</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mystras">Mystras</a> is occupied by a tiny religious community, its buildings are well preserved, and it is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. In antiquity the peak of Taleton was dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helios">Helios</a>, to whom horses were sacrificed, and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>. Today the summit is linked to the Prophet Elias, and each 20 July, his feast day in the Greek Orthodox calendar, a festival with a large bonfire is held at the chapel on the peak. Profitis Ilias is a popular hiking destination, and the European walking route E4 runs along the lower slopes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Taygetus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Geoffrey’s younger son William II of Villehardouin completed the conquest by taking Monemvasia in 1248 after a long siege, and built the fortresses of Mistras, Grand Magne and Beaufort to control the Mani and the Slavic Melingoi of the <b>Taygetus</b> range.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Sparta lay in the valley of the Eurotas River in Laconia, in the south-eastern Peloponnese, between the <b>Taygetus</b> and Parnon mountain ranges.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sparta">Sparta · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
